1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
 In this chapter basics we are going to make a start with the course.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:17,000
 We will cover some basic things without getting too technical but are really giving some flavor of what to expect in the remainder of the course.

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:26,000
 We are going to explore that concept of time value of money which is the basic concept in engineering economy.

4
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:37,000
 We are going to have a look at factors, how can we express these time and interest things that affect money and we are going to talk about nominal and effective interest rates.

5
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,000
 Why do we need that concept time value of money?

6
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,000
 We want to explore the monetary impact of investments that companies do.

7
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
 Obviously when a company and organization makes an investment they expect a return.

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 In computing that return it is important to take into account the time value of money.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
 We will do that through the use of the interest rate.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 What is an interest rate and return? I think this is a very easy step.

11
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
 You can either take the borrower's point of view or the lender's point of view.

12
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
 Look at the example in the grey square.

13
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:29,000
 Suppose that you borrow 10,000 dollars for one full year and you must pay back 10,700 dollars at the end of the year.

14
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,000
 That means that the difference is 700 dollars.

15
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:46,000
 You could say that it is what you as a borrower have to pay or you can say it is what the lender earns by me borrowing that money.

16
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
 If you look at 700 compared to 10,000 we have 7% per year.

17
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,000
 That is how we start.

18
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:02,000
 Why do we need that? We need that because we want another important concept in engineering economy.

19
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:09,000
 We want to have equivalent alternatives or equivalent in the money value of alternatives that we are computing.

20
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
 It is very difficult to compare alternatives.

21
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:22,000
 For instance, I can invest my money in project 1 and then I will have a return of 10,000 within 5 years.

22
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 Or I can invest in project 2 and then in 4 years I will have 8,000 or in 6 years I will have 8,000.

23
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
 It is very difficult to compare.

24
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:39,000
 Basically what we will do is bring back all different alternatives to one point in time.

25
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:51,000
 Which can be now, which we will call later on the present word, or we can go for a moment in time in the future and then we will talk about the future word.

26
00:02:51,000 --> 00:03:03,000
 An equivalent is then if you have $100 now, an interest rate of 6% per year, that means that you will have $106 one year from now.

27
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:11,000
 We already mentioned that interest is important in the time value of money.

28
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
 We have two types of interest. We have a simple interest and a compound interest.

29
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:31,000
 Simple interest for one period is the principal, so the amount you have times the interest rate, for instance, 1000 euros, interest rate of 10%, means that we have 100 euros interest.

30
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:38,000
 If you have more periods then you just have to multiply that amount by the number of periods.

31
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:51,000
 If you are talking about the compound interest, it is getting a little bit more complicated, then we have the principal plus all accrued interest times the interest rate.

32
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:59,000
 Suppose that I make a deposit of 10,000 euros in a savings account for 5 years and the interest rate is 5%.

33
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:06,000
 So how much interest do we earn in the scenario of single interest and the scenario of compound interest?

34
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
 If you look into the single interest case first, what does it mean?

35
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:14,000
 It means that every year I take up the amount of interest earned.

36
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:27,000
 So as you see, I start with 10,000 in year one, I earn 500 in year two, I still have 10,000, I earn again 500 and so on, 5 years long.

37
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:33,000
 So the total amount of interest that I earn is 5 times 500 is 2,500.

38
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:42,000
 In case of the compound interest, what I do is the interest that I earn I deposit in my account.

39
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:52,000
 So you see the first year similar to the same as in the single interest case, I have 10,000 in the bank, I earn 500 interest.

40
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,000
 But instead of taking up that interest, withdrawing that interest, I put it in my account.

41
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,000
 So that means that after two years I have 10,500 in my account.

42
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:07,000
 So the interest that I earn is then interest on my initial amount and interest on the interest of the previous year.

43
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,000
 So I have an interest of 525 and so on and so on.

44
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:23,000
 And after five years, you see I have a sum of 2,763, which is obviously higher than what I had in the single interest case.

45
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 So this simple example shows that it's important to make a distinction between single and compound interest.

46
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:36,000
 The compound interest is one that is of importance for our engineering economy calculations.

47
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:48,000
 In terms of technology and symbols that we will use throughout the course, T represents time.

48
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:54,000
 P is a present sum of money at a time designated as T is zero now.

49
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:01,000
 F is a future amount of money at some point in time later than T is zero, for instance, five years from now.

50
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:09,000
 A is used for an uninterrupted series of equal end of period cash flows, annual cash flows, hence A.

51
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:14,000
 N is a number of interest periods, also called the study period or the horizon.

52
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:19,000
 And I is interest rate or the rate of return per time period, percent.

53
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:25,000
 So these are the symbols that we are going to use. They're pretty self-explaining.

54
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:34,000
 Computational help. We will illustrate that in some exercises that are coming up.

55
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:41,000
 So we can use the tables. There are tables that will help us with our computations and we can use Excel.

56
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:50,000
 In the course we will not use Excel, but of course you can freely use Excel to check the answers to any exercise that you might solve.

57
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:57,000
 The MAR, this is a very important concept, hence the red exclamation mark.

58
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:01,000
 MAR is the minimum attractive rate of return.

59
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:08,000
 We started by stating that companies that invest money, they expect some return on that money.

60
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:19,000
 Well, what do they expect? Well, they expect a return that is either equal or preferably higher than the minimum attractive rate of return that they had in mind.

61
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
 Therefore, that MAR is also called a hurdle rate.

62
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:32,000
 It's the hurdle that if you want an investment needs to take in order to be considered to be a south investment.

63
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:36,000
 The MAR is established by financial managers.

64
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:41,000
 Basically, it's based on the cost of capital and an allowance for risk.

65
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:45,000
 So it's always higher than the return for a safe investment.

66
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:53,000
 In chapter 10, we will go in detail on how companies determine their MAR.

67
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:00,000
 Well, we will not see actual computations, but we will see a whole bunch of factors that are influencing the MAR.

68
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
 It's a very important concept.

69
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:12,000
 This is a case study, as we saw in the exercises for the previous chapter.

70
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:19,000
 And here you see that we had expected returns on capital investment.

71
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:25,000
 And you see in past projects that varied 15%, 5%, 18%.

72
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:30,000
 So really it's something that the company is deciding on.

73
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:38,000
 It's not something you find on the Internet or you find on some, I don't know, in some books of engineer economy or you can ask your bank.

74
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:41,000
 It's really something the company decides for itself.

75
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
 Now types of financing.

76
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:49,000
 We said that the cost of capital is an important aspect of the MAR.

77
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:57,000
 Well, the cost of capital depends on how expensive is it for you to get together the money for that investment.

78
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:07,000
 Basically, you can file it into different sources, your own money, so to speak, or money from outside sources.

79
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:12,000
 So we're talking about equity financing and debt financing responsibility.

80
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
 This is something we will come back to later.

81
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:21,000
 Combination of debt and equity financing is, of course, possible.

82
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:26,000
 And it results in something called the WAC, a weighted average cost of capital.

83
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:30,000
 So the WAC is a basic element of the MAR.

84
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
 A very simple example.

85
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:40,000
 Suppose that you want to finance a music system with a credit card for 40% and the credit card is costing you 18% per year.

86
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:46,000
 And for the rest, 60%, you want to use a saving account earning a 5% per year.

87
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:55,000
 So that means that the WAC will be, well, 40% of 18 plus 60% of 5 will be a 12.2% per year.

88
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
 So important concept.

89
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
 Now inflation.

90
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
 I think you will know what inflation is.

91
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:08,000
 Inflation means that the purchasing power of your money is reduced.

92
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:18,000
 That also means that operating costs will increase and that your rate of return will be reduced.

93
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:23,000
 So definitely something we will need to take into account when we look at investments.

94
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
 Taxes.

95
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:32,000
 Well, unfortunately, it's something we all have to pay, we as individuals, but also companies.

96
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:42,000
 For the main part, we will do before tax cash flow analysis, which is basic.

97
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:49,000
 But then we also have two chapters that will address after tax analysis.

98
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
 Is it something you know?

99
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
 It's not you.

100
00:10:53,000 --> 00:11:00,000
 If you do a replacement study, for instance, before taxes or after taxes, you will use exactly the same.

101
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
 Technique approach.

102
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:11,000
 But after tax, well, as a word said, you will have to include a tax analysis in your computations.

103
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:15,000
 And that can be a little bit complicated sometimes.

104
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
 Now, another important concept.

105
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:22,000
 Well, you see a lot of a lot of important concepts.

106
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,000
 That's why it's a basic chapter.

107
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
 Cash flows concept.

108
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:29,000
 Basic chapter.

109
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:33,000
 Cash flows are in fact what we need.

110
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:39,000
 Cash flows is about the money that is coming into our company and the money that is going out.

111
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:42,000
 Cash flow in, for instance, the revenue.

112
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:47,000
 Think about selling products, you get money in.

113
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:53,000
 Salvage value is what you still get on the market for an old machine.

114
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:59,000
 So suppose that you want to get rid of, I don't know, an old printer.

115
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:06,000
 If it's still worth something on the market, somebody will pay for you, they'll pay maybe 100 euros for it.

116
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
 So that's the salvage value.

117
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
 Cash outflow is money that you have to spend.

118
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:15,000
 That can be an initial purchasing cost.

119
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:16,000
 It can be salaries.

120
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,000
 It can be taxes.

121
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:20,000
 So that are cash outflows.

122
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:28,000
 In practical engineering economy problems, we're talking about investments that we will make today or in the future.

123
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:34,000
 So also the cash flows connected with these investments will be future cash flows.

124
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,000
 So we don't know them yet.

125
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
 What can we do about that?

126
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:46,000
 Well, we can either assume that we know them with certainty, like, well, I know that the annual operating cost will be 10,000 euros a year.

127
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:54,000
 We can take into account probabilities and say, well, probably the annual operating costs will be 10,000 euros a year,

128
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:59,000
 but there's a 20% chance that there will be 12,000 euros a year.

129
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:09,000
 We could take into account a range of possible realistic values, saying, well, annual operating costs will be somewhere between 9,000 and 11,000.

130
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:17,000
 Or we can assume that we know a distribution for our annual operating cost values.

131
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:27,000
 For instance, the annual operating costs will be taken from a triangle distribution, which A is 900, C is 10,000 and B is 13,000 a year.

132
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:36,000
 An assumption that we make is that all cash flows occur at the end of an interest period unless it's stated differently.

133
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:41,000
 That's an important assumption. We need an assumption like that.

134
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:50,000
 Otherwise, we will not be able to make clean calculations taking into account the time value of money.

135
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:57,000
 A tip with these cash flows is that visualizing them in a cash flow diagram will help our analysis.

136
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:03,000
 It will make things clear and it will avoid that we make mistakes.

137
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:08,000
 This is a cash flow diagram. You see it's pretty simple.

138
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:17,000
 We have an x-axis, which represents time, and we will have a y-axis, which represents the cash flows.

139
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:24,000
 A cash flow, which is negative, gets an arrow down. A cash flow, which is positive, gets an arrow up.

140
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:33,000
 So, 0, that's the moment in time that we are now, and then 1, 2, 3, etc. are the coming years.

141
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:40,000
 So, you see, year 1, we will have a cash flow in year 1 at the end of year 1.

142
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:44,000
 That's what we just said.

143
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:51,000
 Exercises we will postpone to the separate exercise session.

144
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:56,000
 You see here these exercises or multiple choice examples.

145
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:06,000
 Now, our notation. We are using the factor notation. It's an ANSI standard.

146
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:14,000
 And as you see, the general form is x slash y, i percent, n.

147
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:21,000
 X represents what is unknown, y represents what is known, i and n represent input parameters.

148
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:26,000
 They can be known or unknown, depending upon the problem, as you will see later in the course.

149
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:35,000
 So, an example, if P is 6 percent 20, is to be read as, well, that's the factor that we need to find F, given a P.

150
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:41,000
 I mean, the factor that we have to multiply with P in order to obtain F.

151
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:47,000
 When we know that the interest rate is 6 percent and the number of time periods to consider is 20.

152
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:54,000
 Now, how to compute these factors? Well, let's have a look at Fp and Pf.

153
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:59,000
 So, if you look at the cash flow diagram, we have an initial amount of P0.

154
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:06,000
 And we want to know the worth of it in years from now.

155
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:10,000
 So, the computation is pretty straightforward, as you see.

156
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:17,000
 If 1 is P plus ip, so that can be written as P times 1 plus i.

157
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:28,000
 The second year, after two years, I mean, we have P times 1 plus i, which is our amount, plus i times P plus 1 plus i, which is the interest.

158
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:33,000
 And that is written as P times 1 plus i square, etc.

159
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:47,000
 So, after in periods, we will have that F is P times 1 plus i to the power n, or the other way around, P is F divided by 1 plus i to the power n.

160
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:54,000
 So, this is the formula on which all the other formulas for factors are based.

161
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:59,000
 So, it's a very important one, and as you see, it's also a very simple one.

162
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:02,000
 Let's have a look at an example. So, we have an engineer.

163
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:15,000
 He received a bonus of 12,000 dollars, and he wants to invest it now and use the money after 24 years to make a down payment on a vacation home.

164
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:26,000
 He can count on a rate of return of 8% per year for each of the 24 years that he plans to leave the money in the bank.

165
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:31,000
 Now, what will be the amount that he can use for the down payment?

166
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:34,000
 Well, we can compute it in different ways.

167
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:43,000
 We can use the formula, so saying F is P times 1 plus i to the power n, and just make the calculation.

168
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:51,000
 We can use the factor notation, and the nice thing about these factors is that in the handbook we have tables.

169
00:17:51,000 --> 00:18:05,000
 We have tables for each percentage and all the factors that you can dream of are there in the columns and in the rows we find the end.

170
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:18,000
 So, if we have a table for 8%, we look after 24 years, we're after the factor Fp, so we can dig it up there, and it's 6.3412.

171
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:32,000
 And another way that we can compute it is by putting the values in the FV formula in Excel and we obtain the same value.

172
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:35,000
 So, different ways of doing things.

173
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:49,000
 In the course we will always use the factor notation because it's very compact and it's easy to have an overview of the whole of computations when using these factor notations.

174
00:18:53,000 --> 00:19:00,000
 Another example, how long will it take for $1000 to double if the interest rate is 5% per year?

175
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:05,000
 Well, here we don't know n, we have to look for n.

176
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:26,000
 We can say that $1000 is $2000 times that factor, so that factor should be 0.5, and then if we look in the table we see that somewhere between 14 and 15 years we will have that 5%.

177
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:33,000
 We can start interpolating and then we come up with 14.2 years.

178
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:54,000
 Other factors, P and Ap. We talked about A already, it's a uniform series factor, so we are talking about an uninterrupted series of equal payments or equal withdrawals.

179
00:19:54,000 --> 00:20:08,000
 And there you see the factor Pa times 1 plus i to the power n minus 1, then everything divided by i times 1 plus i to the power n.

180
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:17,000
 Now how do we get to that formula? Well, I will just show it to you, these computations, to show that it's rather easy, it's really simple algebra.

181
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:32,000
 So we start with just writing out everything, so P is 8 times the factor for the A of 1 year plus 8 times basic formula, 2 years, etc.

182
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:45,000
 So again, that's very basic formula. And then we start manipulating it, we divide everything by 1 plus i, we subtract things.

183
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:59,000
 So very simple computations and then we end up with those factors. Of course if you have to make an exercise you can use that formula, but it's far more easy to look it up in tables.

184
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:13,000
 Let's have a look at this small example, so how much money should Be willing to pay for a guaranteed 600 per year for 9 years starting next year at a rate of return of 16%, a very simple cash flow diagram.

185
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:26,000
 So if we use the formula we just saw, we will say that P is 8 times 1 plus i to the power n minus 1 divided by i times 1 plus i to the power n.

186
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:41,000
 So we have our P immediately. We could do it year by year as you see in the table. We have the present words and we will use the factor for every year separately.

187
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:56,000
 We could also take a detour and say well I will first see what the future amount is of this cash flow series and then I will go back to the present word.

188
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:01,000
 Both are possible, both are correct, one is of course more efficient than the other.

189
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:18,000
 So here an example again with use of the tables. So the president of Formosa Plastics want to know the equivalent future worth of 1 million of capital investment each year for 8 years starting one year from now.

190
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:34,000
 And they earn at a rate of 14% per year. You see again the cash flow diagram. Either we use the formula, we make the computations or we use the factors and you see the table there.

191
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:48,000
 We have the table for 14%, we have to look at 8 years and we have to look for the factor FA. So we find it and it is rather simple to make computations.

192
00:22:48,000 --> 00:23:17,000
 So another question, how much money must someone deposit every year from now at 5.5% in order to accumulate $6000 7 years from now. We don't have a table for 5.5% so we can either use the formula that you see there to the bottom left of the slide or we can take the table for 5% and 6% and make a simple interpolation.

193
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:38,000
 And then we end up with a factor of 0.12098 and we can multiply that with the 6,000 and we see that well there is an annual amount of 725.76 per year needed.

194
00:23:38,000 --> 00:24:05,000
 Gradient factors. Sometimes cash flows are increasing or decreasing by a constant amount. Every year there will be an extra withdrawal or every year there will be an extra income and that extra will always be a constant amount.

195
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:24,000
 So that is what you see in the cash flow profile. So we have a base amount and we have a gradient. So we have a cash flow in year N is the base amount plus N minus 1 times G and G is that constant amount.

196
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:40,000
 And then you see the formula. Again, I just show it to you. We will not go through it. It's basic algebra. Here again you see that it's a pretty straightforward computation.

197
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:53,000
 And of course we can also compute the factor for the uniform series and for the future world.

198
00:24:53,000 --> 00:25:15,000
 An example. So three counties in Florida have agreed to pool tax resources for bridge refurbishment. A recent meeting the county engineers estimated that a total of $500,000 will be deposited end of next year in an account for the repair of old and safety questionable bridges.

199
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:29,000
 And further they estimated the deposits will increase by 100,000 per year for only nine years thereafter and then cease. So what we want to know is the present world and the annual world.

200
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:46,000
 So this is a cash flow diagram. We have this 500,000 which we start and then we have the 100,000 that is coming on top of it every year.

201
00:25:46,000 --> 00:26:05,000
 So here we see the computations. So we have here the base amount, so the 500,000 and then we have the gradient. So we have to split it up.

202
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:23,000
 So that's something you keep in mind. Sometimes if you just use the factors you're a little bit confused about what they mean. Think that it's just computing or just multiplying the starting amount or just multiplying the gradient amount.

203
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:47,000
 Well, be careful. You have a base amount and you have a gradient. So you have to split it up. Same thing can be done for the annual series. Of course the annual series, the 500, we don't have to multiply with anything because it's already an annual amount and then we just have the gradient amount that we have to multiply.

204
00:26:47,000 --> 00:27:00,000
 Of course we could have made our life easy by once we have this amount by going directly to the annual amount by then using the AP factor. So that's also possible.

205
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:17,000
 Sometimes indeed cash flows increase or decrease every year but not by a constant amount but by a constant percentage. For instance, a typical example is maintenance costs that get a little bit more expensive every year.

206
00:27:17,000 --> 00:27:38,000
 Again, we have our formula that you see. Again, the computations that are pretty straightforward. Let's take a look at this example. So we're talking about innovating existing water sports, right?

207
00:27:38,000 --> 00:28:01,000
 Modification cost is $8,000 now. We expect that it can last for six years and after the six years we'll still get a salvage value of $1,300 meaning that we will be able to sell whatever is left of this modification for $1,300.

208
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:22,000
 Of course we have to take into account maintenance costs which is expected to be $1,700 the first year and increase by 11% per year. So this is again a negative cash flow as you see on the cash flow diagram as is of course the initial investment and the only positive cash flow is the salvage value.

209
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:39,000
 We also know the time period, the horizon, it's six years and we are given the 8% interest rate that we need to take into account. So we have everything, we have our interest rate, we have our horizon, we have our cash flow so we can do the computations.

210
00:28:39,000 --> 00:29:04,000
 What do we have if we look at the formula? If we look at the formula, well we have our cash flow that we are after. We want to know the present world so that means at $8,000 we don't have to do anything about it because it's already the current time that is given.

211
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:33,000
 Here we have the $1,300 of our salvage value, this is a minus, this is a plus, $1,300 times the factor PF, 8% 6, PF we are after P we know F and then we have our gradient and we just here we use the gradient formula and that means finally we end up with an amount of minus $17,305.85.

212
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:59,000
 Of course, most real life cash flows do not fit exactly in the basic formula assumptions so we will have to make combinations.

213
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:16,000
 Here we have an example, so it's a purchase, it's about a purchase for CAD software, $5,000 now and annual payments of $500 for 6 years starting not next year but 3 years from now for annual upgrades.

214
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:31,000
 And if you consider the horizon of now, 3 years from now, 6 years we have 8 years in total, that's our horizon and the interest rate is 8%.

215
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:48,000
 Okay, so let's have a look. If we have this, we have this, so it's a simple annual flow, so we can use this formula.

216
00:30:48,000 --> 00:31:03,000
 It's 500 times PA, we are after P, we know 8% 6 years, so we can compute that but then we have this amount.

217
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:23,000
 So this amount is still not what we are after, we want to know it now, so we have to take into account this time gap and this time gap we can take into account by multiplying what we just found by the PF 8% 2.

218
00:31:23,000 --> 00:31:38,000
 And $5,000, well $5,000 is already current money I would say so it's okay. So and then if we bring together those three we end up with a total amount of $6,981.

219
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:54,000
 Let's have a look at a slightly more complex example. We know that we are talking about a 7% interest rate per year, we see a horizon of 12 years and we also see different cash flows.

220
00:31:54,000 --> 00:32:23,000
 Cash flow diagram, let's see, a little bit of variation, this is summarized in the table below. So we have P1, an investment with a base amount of $2,000, gradient of $500, a period of 5 years, it starts in year 1, we have a withdrawal sequence of $5,000 with a gradient of $-1,000, again 5 years starting in year 1.

221
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:38,000
 And then we have a withdrawal sequence of $1,000, no gradient so twice the amount of $1,000 and periods is 2 years and we are talking about years 11 and 12.

222
00:32:38,000 --> 00:33:07,000
 So I've tried to make an animated slide in order to make things a little bit more clear. Okay so we start with this series of cash flows. It's an investment sequence with a base amount of $2,000, a gradient of $500, it is 5 so we will need P1, we will need PG in order to have that amount P1.

223
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:22,000
 Now this withdrawal cash flow range, base amount $5,000, gradient of $-1,000, it is 5 so we will need PG in order to come to P2.

224
00:33:22,000 --> 00:33:35,000
 Then we have our second withdrawal sequence which is $1,000 twice as we just said when we looked at the table. So we will need again PG and then P3.

225
00:33:35,000 --> 00:34:02,000
 Okay now we will also need to, well not convert but to take into account that PW that we computed that it's not exactly already at moment 0 so we will have to use a factor in order to bring it to time 0.

226
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:30,000
 Let us now have a look at the formulas. So let's start with the P1 here which are the orange cash flows. So the present worth of deposits is 2,000 times PA 7% 5 years, pretty straight forward plus the 500 times the factor PG 7% 5 years so we are fine with that.

227
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:53,000
 Then we move to the first withdrawal sequence which is in the green ellipse. Okay so if you look here we have 5,000 times PA 7% 5 that's fine, minus 1,000 PG 7% 5 that's fine.

228
00:34:53,000 --> 00:35:09,000
 But then we are only in year 5 and of course we need to go back to our current moment so time 0 so we still need to multiply by an extra factor PF 7% 5.

229
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:35,000
 In a similar way for the second withdrawal series we have 1,000 times PA 7% 2 but then we are only in year 10 so from year 10 we have to go to current moment time 0 so we have to multiply by PF 7% 10.

230
00:35:35,000 --> 00:35:45,000
 Then if we add up our PW with our P1 we get our final answer which we are interested in.

231
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:59,000
 So note we work with these compact notations that's what I said earlier so we work with the factor notation the PA the PG the PF etc.

232
00:35:59,000 --> 00:36:06,000
 So it's important that you know how to read and how to use these factor notations.

233
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:35,000
 Another way if you don't use the factor notations would be to use the long way so we could in fact make a long list of all the 12 years and show the cash or fill in the cash flows in the table and then use the factors PF and then add everything up and we would end up of course with the same result.

234
00:36:35,000 --> 00:36:37,000
 But it's much more work.

235
00:36:37,000 --> 00:36:44,000
 The last part of this basics chapter is about nominal and effective interest rates.

236
00:36:44,000 --> 00:36:52,000
 The nominal interest rate R is an interest rate that does not include any consideration of compounding.

237
00:36:52,000 --> 00:37:00,000
 And if you remember from the beginning of this chapter compounding we talk about compounding if we talk about interest rate on interest.

238
00:37:00,000 --> 00:37:08,000
 Interest rate per period times the number of periods for instance 8% per year.

239
00:37:08,000 --> 00:37:17,000
 So here you see some examples we can have of course we can talk about 1% or 1.5% per month.

240
00:37:17,000 --> 00:37:20,000
 We can talk for six months etc.

241
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:23,000
 So it's a nominal interest rate.

242
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:31,000
 Effective interest rate IA is the actual rate that applies for a stated period of time.

243
00:37:31,000 --> 00:37:36,000
 It accounts for the compounding of the interest during the time period of the corresponding nominal rate.

244
00:37:36,000 --> 00:37:41,000
 And that's the one that really matters to properly account for the time value of money.

245
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:44,000
 And its notation is IA.

246
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:46,000
 A for actual.

247
00:37:46,000 --> 00:37:53,000
 You see some examples like 4% per year compounded monthly and some other examples in the table.

248
00:37:53,000 --> 00:37:59,000
 Now how do we go from one type of interest to the other?

249
00:37:59,000 --> 00:38:04,000
 Well time period T is the period over which the interest is expressed.

250
00:38:04,000 --> 00:38:08,000
 It's always stated 1% per month, 10% per year.

251
00:38:08,000 --> 00:38:15,000
 The compounding period is the shortest time unit over which interest is charged or earned like 8% per year.

252
00:38:15,000 --> 00:38:17,000
 Compounded monthly.

253
00:38:17,000 --> 00:38:23,000
 Of course we can also talk about the compounding frequency which is the number of times M.

254
00:38:23,000 --> 00:38:26,000
 That compounding occurs within a time period.

255
00:38:26,000 --> 00:38:31,000
 For instance if you have 10% per year compounded monthly then M is 12.

256
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:34,000
 There you see the formula.

257
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:44,000
 An effective rate per compounding period I is R per time period T divided by the number of compounding periods M per T.

258
00:38:44,000 --> 00:38:48,000
 So we have R over M.

259
00:38:48,000 --> 00:38:55,000
 In the previous examples all the interest rates had T and CP being 1.

260
00:38:55,000 --> 00:38:59,000
 So M was 1 so the interest rates were effective rates.

261
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:02,000
 Here you see some illustration.

262
00:39:02,000 --> 00:39:04,000
 So 9% per year.

263
00:39:04,000 --> 00:39:10,000
 Compounding period is quarter.

264
00:39:10,000 --> 00:39:16,000
 So M is 4 and we get effective rates of 2.25.

265
00:39:16,000 --> 00:39:20,000
 Effective annual interest rates.

266
00:39:20,000 --> 00:39:23,000
 So let's look at that.

267
00:39:23,000 --> 00:39:26,000
 Notation R is nominal interest rate.

268
00:39:26,000 --> 00:39:28,000
 M is the number of compounding periods.

269
00:39:28,000 --> 00:39:31,000
 I should say nominal interest rate per year.

270
00:39:31,000 --> 00:39:33,000
 Number of compounding periods per year.

271
00:39:33,000 --> 00:39:37,000
 I is the effective interest rate per compounding period.

272
00:39:37,000 --> 00:39:41,000
 So we can express then an F in two ways.

273
00:39:41,000 --> 00:39:45,000
 We can say F is P plus IAP.

274
00:39:45,000 --> 00:39:54,000
 Or we can have a look at F as being P times 1 plus I to the power M.

275
00:39:54,000 --> 00:40:02,000
 So if we combine those two we end up with a formula for IAP being 1 plus IAP.

276
00:40:02,000 --> 00:40:04,000
 Let's briefly look at this example.

277
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:08,000
 So we have Shaki who obtained a new credit card from the national bank.

278
00:40:08,000 --> 00:40:12,000
 With a stated rate of 18% per year compounded monthly.

279
00:40:12,000 --> 00:40:16,000
 And there is a balance of 1000 dollars at the beginning of the year.

280
00:40:16,000 --> 00:40:24,000
 And now we want to know what the effective annual rate is and how much she would owe to the bank after one year.

281
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:26,000
 We have the computation.

282
00:40:26,000 --> 00:40:36,000
 Before we go to solving this question let's maybe draw your attention to the fact that it's important that they state that no payments have been made during the year.

283
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:40,000
 Otherwise we would have another situation as you will see in upcoming examples.

284
00:40:40,000 --> 00:40:46,000
 So I will just go through the numbers and then we will go to the next slide.

285
00:40:46,000 --> 00:40:49,000
 So they stated no payments have been made during the year.

286
00:40:49,000 --> 00:40:53,000
 Otherwise we would have another situation as you will see in upcoming examples.

287
00:40:53,000 --> 00:40:55,000
 So R is 18% per year.

288
00:40:55,000 --> 00:40:57,000
 It's nominal interest rate.

289
00:40:57,000 --> 00:40:58,000
 It's compounded monthly.

290
00:40:58,000 --> 00:41:02,000
 It means that we have 12 compounding periods being M.

291
00:41:02,000 --> 00:41:10,000
 And our effective interest rate I is then R divided by M is 18% divided by 12 is 1.5% per month.

292
00:41:10,000 --> 00:41:15,000
 The effective annual interest rate is given by the formula we just derived.

293
00:41:15,000 --> 00:41:22,000
 1 plus I to the power M minus 1 and gives us 0.19562.

294
00:41:22,000 --> 00:41:27,000
 The amount out after one year, well that's our good old formula.

295
00:41:27,000 --> 00:41:41,000
 If it's P times 1 plus IA to the power 1 and that gives us an amount of 1,195.62 dollars.

296
00:41:45,000 --> 00:41:52,000
 And here again you see an illustration of the effective annual interest rates.

297
00:41:52,000 --> 00:41:59,000
 Now of course we could have a look at effective interest rates for any period of time.

298
00:41:59,000 --> 00:42:06,000
 And then we are talking about the compounding periods and the payment periods that do not necessarily coincide.

299
00:42:06,000 --> 00:42:14,000
 For instance a company deposits money each month into an account that pays a nominal interest rate of 14% per year.

300
00:42:14,000 --> 00:42:16,000
 Compounded semi-annually.

301
00:42:16,000 --> 00:42:22,000
 So that means that we have a CP of 6 and a payment period of 1.

302
00:42:22,000 --> 00:42:29,000
 And then again we are going back to our formulas.

303
00:42:29,000 --> 00:42:35,000
 Then we have the effective I which is 1 plus R over M to the power M minus 1.

304
00:42:35,000 --> 00:42:39,000
 I think it's rather familiar to you by now.

305
00:42:40,000 --> 00:42:53,000
 Here we have a table that is illustrating how the values differ if we change the nominal, semi-annual, quarterly, etc.

306
00:42:53,000 --> 00:42:59,000
 The last column we will come back to continuously because that's a bit of a special case.

307
00:42:59,000 --> 00:43:03,000
 So here we have an example.

308
00:43:03,000 --> 00:43:05,000
 So Jenis is an engineer.

309
00:43:05,000 --> 00:43:13,000
 She purchased stock for 6.90 per share and sold it one year later for 13.14.

310
00:43:13,000 --> 00:43:19,000
 And now the question is, well, what did she earn?

311
00:43:19,000 --> 00:43:27,000
 And can you express that in terms of annual rates and effective rates for quarterly compounding and for monthly compounding?

312
00:43:27,000 --> 00:43:31,000
 Okay, we will not look at commission fees.

313
00:43:32,000 --> 00:43:35,000
 So here again I wrote it down.

314
00:43:35,000 --> 00:43:43,000
 So the effective annual rate of return in fact is IA that has a compounding period of one year.

315
00:43:43,000 --> 00:43:51,000
 So then we have IA is the amount of increase per one year divided by the original price expressed in percentage.

316
00:43:51,000 --> 00:43:55,000
 So we get 90.43% per year.

317
00:43:55,000 --> 00:44:02,000
 Now the formulas that we need for the effective rate for quarterly compounding or monthly compounding are given there.

318
00:44:02,000 --> 00:44:05,000
 Well, we know IA in this case.

319
00:44:05,000 --> 00:44:07,000
 We are after I.

320
00:44:07,000 --> 00:44:20,000
 So IA is 1 plus I to the power M minus 1, which means that I is given by IA plus 1 to the power 1 over M minus 1.

321
00:44:20,000 --> 00:44:30,000
 So and then we end up with for the quarterly compounding with 17% and for the monthly compounding with 5%.

322
00:44:30,000 --> 00:44:45,000
 So then there's a word of warning that, of course, in this case, you cannot just simple divide I by 4, for instance, to get the quarterly compounding interest.

323
00:44:46,000 --> 00:44:49,000
 Another example.

324
00:44:49,000 --> 00:44:53,000
 It's not it's nothing new.

325
00:44:53,000 --> 00:45:04,000
 I mean, we have a company called Vistion and they received bits from potential suppliers and they want to compare them.

326
00:45:04,000 --> 00:45:13,000
 And the thing is that it's a bit difficult to compare because they well, bit one is 9% per year compounded quarterly.

327
00:45:13,000 --> 00:45:16,000
 Bit two is 3% per quarter compounded quarterly.

328
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:20,000
 Bit three is 8.8% per year compounded monthly.

329
00:45:20,000 --> 00:45:22,000
 So it's a bit confusing to compare.

330
00:45:22,000 --> 00:45:31,000
 So we would like to compare them in a way that we understand which is the best, which is the worst.

331
00:45:33,000 --> 00:45:36,000
 So here we have the computations.

332
00:45:36,000 --> 00:45:40,000
 But let's I again wrote it out a little bit more clear.

333
00:45:40,000 --> 00:45:56,000
 So here you have a blow up of the cash flow diagrams with CP one quarter, PP six months for bit one and two and for bit three, we have a PP of six months, but we have a CP of one month.

334
00:46:00,000 --> 00:46:07,000
 So basically, this is the same as in the copy of the handbook that was on previous slide.

335
00:46:07,000 --> 00:46:15,000
 The reason I wrote it out is to show that, well, it's not difficult, but it's easy to make mistakes.

336
00:46:15,000 --> 00:46:23,000
 So sometimes it pays off to take a long way here and to write like bit one is 9% per year compounded quarterly.

337
00:46:23,000 --> 00:46:25,000
 PP is six months.

338
00:46:25,000 --> 00:46:27,000
 R is 9% per year.

339
00:46:27,000 --> 00:46:31,000
 That's 4.5% per six months and M is two quarters per six months.

340
00:46:31,000 --> 00:46:34,000
 And then we fill out in the in the in the formula.

341
00:46:34,000 --> 00:46:38,000
 Then we see that bit three seems to be the most interesting one.

342
00:46:38,000 --> 00:46:40,000
 Same for effective annual rate.

343
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:44,000
 Take the time to write things out so that you don't make any mistakes.

344
00:46:44,000 --> 00:46:48,000
 And again, bit three seems to be have the lowest annual rate.

345
00:46:50,000 --> 00:46:59,000
 And we can start playing around further with these equivalence relations for PP and CP.

346
00:47:00,000 --> 00:47:05,000
 Here you have what you have to do if PP is less than CP or PP is larger than CP.

347
00:47:06,000 --> 00:47:14,000
 There's nothing new, so I leave it at you to explore further these things.

348
00:47:14,000 --> 00:47:17,000
 We have you have the formulas, you have examples.

349
00:47:18,000 --> 00:47:20,000
 It will come back in the workshop.

350
00:47:20,000 --> 00:47:23,000
 So let's skip it for now.

351
00:47:23,000 --> 00:47:29,000
 Now, I think you are quite capable of having a look at it on your own.

352
00:47:31,000 --> 00:47:37,000
 Maybe let me just draw your attention to this special case, the continuous compounding.

353
00:47:37,000 --> 00:47:42,000
 So we're talking about a business situation in which there are very large number of cash flows every day.

354
00:47:42,000 --> 00:47:46,000
 So it's like we are doing continuous compounding.

355
00:47:46,000 --> 00:47:56,000
 So if we start from the formula that we know by now that one plus R over M to the power M minus one is I.

356
00:47:56,000 --> 00:48:00,000
 And we take the limit for M going to infinity.

357
00:48:00,000 --> 00:48:07,000
 Of course, because we have a lot of say an infinite amount of compounding periods.

358
00:48:07,000 --> 00:48:12,000
 We end up with the formula that I is equal to E to the power R minus one.

359
00:48:13,000 --> 00:48:17,000
 And here again, you have an example of that.

360
00:48:18,000 --> 00:48:25,000
 An interesting situation can occur when interest rates are not constant over time, but they vary over time.

361
00:48:26,000 --> 00:48:29,000
 I think you already guess what you have to do then.

362
00:48:29,000 --> 00:48:34,000
 You have to be careful and make sure that you don't make any mistakes.

363
00:48:34,000 --> 00:48:39,000
 It's really not difficult, but it can be cumbersome task to perform.

364
00:48:40,000 --> 00:48:46,000
 Maybe a simple example to illustrate to make sure that there are no misunderstandings here.

365
00:48:46,000 --> 00:48:49,000
 I re-drew the cash flow diagram.

366
00:48:49,000 --> 00:48:55,000
 So we have in year one, we have 70,000, sorry, in year two, we have 70,000.

367
00:48:55,000 --> 00:48:59,000
 25,000 in three and 25,000 in four.

368
00:48:59,000 --> 00:49:04,000
 During the first two years, we have a 7% interest rate that we have to take into account.

369
00:49:04,000 --> 00:49:10,000
 We want 9% in the third year and then 10% in the fourth year.

370
00:49:14,000 --> 00:49:18,000
 What do we have if we want to compute the present worth?

371
00:49:18,000 --> 00:49:23,000
 Well, we have the 7,000 times PA 7% 2.

372
00:49:23,000 --> 00:49:29,000
 For the 35,000, we have the 35,000 times PF 9% 1.

373
00:49:29,000 --> 00:49:38,000
 We go from year three to year two and then we have to multiply by PF 7% 2 to get from year two to year zero.

374
00:49:38,000 --> 00:49:44,000
 Because we can't explicitly with just one factor because our interest rate is varying.

375
00:49:44,000 --> 00:49:47,000
 Of course, for the 10% we have a similar thing.

376
00:49:47,000 --> 00:49:58,000
 We have 25,000 times PF 10% 1 year times PF 9% 1 year times PF 7% 2 years.

377
00:49:58,000 --> 00:50:05,000
 So again, it's definitely not difficult, but you have to be careful not to make any computational mistakes.

378
00:50:05,000 --> 00:50:14,000
 Exercises again, we postpone to the exercise session or to the workshop.

379
00:50:16,000 --> 00:50:21,000
 So wrap up. What did we learn in this chapter that is important?

380
00:50:21,000 --> 00:50:26,000
 What is important is that we talked about the time value of money, which is an essential concept.

